# G058 — Movie Store

*v2. See `movie-store.md` for prose: rental lifecycle, overdue as time-derived
query, per-movie and per-customer history views.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Nightly Overdue Sweep

[nightly-overdue-sweep @now
    IN ORDER TO
        every night find overdue rentals
        notify each affected customer
        and calculate late fees for kathryn to review

    @overdue <- [store[overdue {now: @now}]]
    @by-customer <- @overdue grouped by :customer-id

    concurrent [
        for @(cust, rentals) in @by-customer
            <- @sarah{notify customer: @cust, rentals: @rentals, severity: "late"}
        <- @kathryn{assess fees for overdue: @overdue}
    ]
    join

    where
        every customer was notified
        and total fees are non-negative
        || -> #sweep-incomplete @resistance:reason
]

---

## Verb Surface

[store
    new
    add-movie        {movie}
    add-customer     {customer}
    rent             {movie-id, customer-id, duration-days}   ;; || no-copies-available
    return           {rental-id}
    copies-available {movie-id}
    active-rentals   {customer-id}
    overdue          {now}
    movie-history    {movie-id}
    customer-history {customer-id}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: overdue is derived, not stored

[store[overdue {now: @now}
    where
        a rental is overdue if its due-at is before @now and it's not yet returned
        computed on demand, not stored as a boolean field
        so the answer updates automatically as time advances
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    @s <- [store[new]]
    [store[add-movie    {movie:    {id: 1, title: "Dune", copies: 2}}]]
    [store[add-customer {customer: {id: 1, name: "Alice"}}]]

    @r <- [store[rent {movie-id: 1, customer-id: 1, duration-days: 3}]]
    -> [store[copies-available {movie-id: 1}]]   ;; -> 1

    -> [store[overdue {now: @r:due-at + 1 day}]]:count   ;; -> 1

    [store[return {rental-id: @r:id}]]
    -> [store[copies-available {movie-id: 1}]]   ;; -> 2
]
